React Server Component vs Client Component in Next.js

=>    react ka jitna b kam hum agr nextjs me krna chahty ha tu sab sy phly hamai us component ko "client component" bnana prta ha,
=>    react ka jo b code ha hum usy client component me likhy gy aur backend ka jo b code ha hum usy server componnet me likhy gy
React server component ka use below scenarios me karain gy
a-use it when we have to fetch data and only have to represent it
b-use it when we want to hide url
c-Use it when we want to load data even before that component open while in react ,ui is rendered then make api call and then load the data
=>if  a client component imports another component that imported component becomes a client component too even if it does not have use "use client"
=>by default all the files including layout and pages are all react server components except 1 file which is error.js 



=>client component ko kabhi b async ni bna skty
--------------------------------------------------
1️⃣ React Server Components (RSC)

Runs on the server, never in the browser.

Can fetch data directly from a database, filesystem, or API without exposing secrets.

Returns HTML + serialized props to the client.(Serialized props:The data fetched is converted to JSON-like format so the client/browser can understand it.This converatable json data ko serialized props kehty ha).

Cannot use client-side hooks like useState, useEffect, or browser APIs (window, document).

Very fast for initial load because the server does the heavy lifting and sends ready-to-render HTML.

Example:

// server component (default)
const Products = async () => {
  const res = await fetch("https://api.example.com/products");
  const products = await res.json();

  return (
    <ul>
      {products.map((p) => (
        <li key={p.id}>{p.name}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
};


This fetch happens on the server, the browser only gets HTML.

2️⃣ React Client Components

Runs in the browser, same as normal React.

Can use state, effects, browser APIs, event handlers.

Can still receive props from a parent server component, but the server does not run this code.

Useful for interactivity: buttons, forms, modals, toggles.

To mark a component as client-side:

'use client'

import { useState } from "react";

const Counter = () => {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Count: {count}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>
    </div>
  );
};

export default Counter;


This runs entirely in the browser, React behaves just like normal React.

The server does not execute this component — it only sends the server component HTML to the client, and React hydrates the client component there.
