Incremental Static Regeneration(ISR)

Why we need it?
When we are in production,hamaray data jo let suppose hamai db sy a rha ha ,us me koi update huti ha tu dynamic page me wo changes reflect hu gi mgr static page me previous data ko he hold rkhy ga which means static page me changes reflect ni hugi.Static me ye issue resolve krny k liye ISR ka use krty ha.ISR hamary static page ko b dynamic bnany  ki power rkhta ha.

export const revalidate=20;

ISR Simple & Accurate Explanation (Final Version)

When the developer builds the project at 12:00 PM, Next.js generates the static page and immediately starts the revalidate timer (for example, 10 seconds). If a user visits before the timer expires, they see the old static data. Once the timer expires, the next user who visits still sees the old page, but Next.js realizes the timer is over and quietly fetches updated data in the background. After this background fetch is finished, Next.js updates the stored static page, and the new version will only become visible when the next user loads or refreshes the page. At that same moment, a new revalidate timer starts again. This cycle continues forever—users always get instant responses, and the page gets refreshed only after the timer expires and a user triggers regeneration.


